      "content": "Scientists often rely on several frameworks to justify vaccination mandates, including epidemiological modeling, cost-benefit analysis, and probabilistic thought experiments. They may employ quantitative methods, such as dynamic transmission models (e.g., SEIR models), to estimate the potential benefits of vaccination on public health outcomes. Additionally, they might reference historical examples of successful vaccination campaigns, like smallpox eradication, to demonstrate the efficacy of vaccination policies. They may also cite peer-reviewed studies on the individual and collective health benefits of vaccinated populations, as well as the risk of vaccine-preventable diseases persisting or re-emerging if vaccination rates fall below a certain threshold.",
